window.aifExamData.domain5 = {"id":5,"title":"AIF-C01: Domain 5 Test Exam","subtitle":"Security, Compliance, and Governance for AI Solutions","weight":"14%","questions":[{"q":"A GenAI application needs permission to read only one approved S3 bucket and invoke one specific model. Which AWS service provides the primary mechanism for enforcing these least-privilege permissions?","opts":["AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","AWS KMS","Amazon Macie","Amazon Bedrock Guardrails"],"ans":[0],"why":"IAM roles and policies control AWS permissions.","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"A. AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","correctReason":"IAM roles and policies control AWS permissions. The key signal is **permissions or least privilege**.","incorrectReasons":{"1":"Creates and manages encryption keys used to protect data. Here, the deciding detail is permissions or least privilege, so this option describes a different need.","2":"Discovers and helps protect sensitive data, especially in Amazon S3. Here, the deciding detail is permissions or least privilege, so this option describes a different need.","3":"Provides configurable GenAI safeguards such as content filters, denied topics, and sensitive-information controls. Here, the deciding detail is permissions or least privilege, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"**permissions or least privilege**","examTip":"Signal: **permissions or least privilege**. Decision rule: IAM. Contrast: KMS manages encryption keys; Macie discovers sensitive data; Guardrails filters GenAI content.","transferCheck":"","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"**permissions or least privilege**","think":"IAM","contrast":"KMS manages encryption keys; Macie discovers sensitive data; Guardrails filters GenAI content."}},"status":"substantially revised","task":"5.1","taskName":"Explain methods to secure AI systems","difficulty":"moderate","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: Best Practices for Secure Data Engineering","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/Best-Practices-for-Secure-Data-Engineering/page"}},{"q":"A company encrypts sensitive AI training data and must centrally create, control, and rotate the encryption keys. Which AWS service is MOST appropriate?","opts":["AWS Secrets Manager","AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","AWS KMS","Amazon Macie"],"ans":[2],"why":"AWS KMS manages encryption keys.","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"C. AWS KMS","correctReason":"AWS KMS manages encryption keys. The key signal is **create/control encryption keys**.","incorrectReasons":{"0":"Stores, retrieves, and rotates secrets such as credentials; it is not a general encryption-key-management replacement for KMS. Here, the deciding detail is create/control encryption keys, so this option describes a different need.","1":"Controls AWS identities, roles, policies, permissions, and least-privilege authorization. Here, the deciding detail is create/control encryption keys, so this option describes a different need.","3":"Discovers and helps protect sensitive data, especially in Amazon S3. Here, the deciding detail is create/control encryption keys, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"**create/control encryption keys**","examTip":"Signal: **create/control encryption keys**. Decision rule: KMS. Contrast: Secrets Manager stores credentials/secrets; IAM controls authorization.","transferCheck":"","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"**create/control encryption keys**","think":"KMS","contrast":"Secrets Manager stores credentials/secrets; IAM controls authorization."}},"status":"substantially revised","task":"5.1","taskName":"Explain methods to secure AI systems","difficulty":"moderate","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: Best Practices for Secure Data Engineering","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/Best-Practices-for-Secure-Data-Engineering/page"}},{"q":"Before using an S3 data lake for model training, a company wants to discover sensitive information such as personally identifiable information (PII). Which AWS service BEST fits this requirement?","opts":["AWS KMS","AWS CloudTrail","AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","Amazon Macie"],"ans":[3],"why":"Amazon Macie discovers sensitive data in Amazon S3.","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"D. Amazon Macie","correctReason":"Amazon Macie discovers sensitive data in Amazon S3. The key signal is **discover PII or sensitive data in S3**.","incorrectReasons":{"0":"Creates and manages encryption keys used to protect data. Here, the deciding detail is discover PII or sensitive data in S3, so this option describes a different need.","1":"Records AWS API and account activity for audit and investigation. Here, the deciding detail is discover PII or sensitive data in S3, so this option describes a different need.","2":"Controls AWS identities, roles, policies, permissions, and least-privilege authorization. Here, the deciding detail is discover PII or sensitive data in S3, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"**discover PII or sensitive data in S3**","examTip":"Signal: **discover PII or sensitive data in S3**. Decision rule: Macie. Contrast: KMS encrypts; CloudTrail audits activity.","transferCheck":"","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"**discover PII or sensitive data in S3**","think":"Macie","contrast":"KMS encrypts; CloudTrail audits activity."}},"status":"substantially revised","task":"5.1","taskName":"Explain methods to secure AI systems","difficulty":"moderate","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: Best Practices for Secure Data Engineering","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/Best-Practices-for-Secure-Data-Engineering/page"}},{"q":"A regulated workload must access a supported AWS AI service privately from a VPC without sending traffic over the public internet. Which AWS networking feature is MOST appropriate?","opts":["AWS PrivateLink","A public service endpoint protected by TLS","Amazon CloudFront","An IAM role without a private network path"],"ans":[0],"why":"AWS PrivateLink provides private connectivity to supported services.","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"A. AWS PrivateLink","correctReason":"AWS PrivateLink provides private connectivity to supported services. The key signal is **private VPC-to-service connectivity without public internet traversal**.","incorrectReasons":{"1":"Can be secure with TLS but does not satisfy a requirement to keep traffic off the public internet. Here, the deciding detail is private VPC-to-service connectivity without public internet traversal, so this option describes a different need.","2":"Is a content delivery network and edge service; it does not provide the same private VPC endpoint pattern as PrivateLink. Here, the deciding detail is private VPC-to-service connectivity without public internet traversal, so this option describes a different need.","3":"Can authorize access but does not create the requested private network path. Here, the deciding detail is private VPC-to-service connectivity without public internet traversal, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"**private VPC-to-service connectivity without public internet traversal**","examTip":"Signal: **private VPC-to-service connectivity without public internet traversal**. Decision rule: PrivateLink. Contrast: IAM authorizes actions but does not create a private network path.","transferCheck":"","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"**private VPC-to-service connectivity without public internet traversal**","think":"PrivateLink","contrast":"IAM authorizes actions but does not create a private network path."}},"status":"substantially revised","task":"5.1","taskName":"Explain methods to secure AI systems","difficulty":"challenging","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: Best Practices for Secure Data Engineering","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/Best-Practices-for-Secure-Data-Engineering/page"}},{"q":"A GenAI application must filter harmful content, denied topics, and sensitive information from model interactions. Which AWS capability is MOST appropriate?","opts":["AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","Policy in Amazon Bedrock AgentCore","Amazon Bedrock Guardrails","Amazon Macie"],"ans":[2],"why":"Bedrock Guardrails applies safety and topic controls.","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"C. Amazon Bedrock Guardrails","correctReason":"Bedrock Guardrails applies safety and topic controls. The key signal is **managed access to foundation models without operating model-serving infrastructure**.","incorrectReasons":{"0":"Controls AWS identities, roles, policies, permissions, and least-privilege authorization. Here, the deciding detail is managed access to foundation models without operating model-serving infrastructure, so this option describes a different need.","1":"Provides deterministic authorization policies for agent tool calls through AgentCore Gateway. Here, the deciding detail is managed access to foundation models without operating model-serving infrastructure, so this option describes a different need.","3":"Discovers and helps protect sensitive data, especially in Amazon S3. Here, the deciding detail is managed access to foundation models without operating model-serving infrastructure, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"**managed access to foundation models without operating model-serving infrastructure**","examTip":"Signal: **managed access to foundation models without operating model-serving infrastructure**. Decision rule: Bedrock. Contrast: SageMaker AI is the broader choice when custom training and lifecycle control dominate.","transferCheck":"If the team instead needed to train and tune custom ML models with broad lifecycle control, which AWS service would become the stronger fit?","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"**managed access to foundation models without operating model-serving infrastructure**","think":"Bedrock","contrast":"SageMaker AI is the broader choice when custom training and lifecycle control dominate."}},"status":"substantially revised","task":"5.1","taskName":"Explain methods to secure AI systems","difficulty":"straightforward","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: Best Practices for Secure Data Engineering","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/Best-Practices-for-Secure-Data-Engineering/page"}},{"q":"An AI agent needs to authenticate to approved AWS and third-party systems and obtain credentials securely when invoking tools. Which AWS capability is MOST relevant?","opts":["Policy in Amazon Bedrock AgentCore","Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Identity","AWS Secrets Manager used by the application directly","Amazon Bedrock Guardrails"],"ans":[1],"why":"AgentCore Identity helps agents securely authenticate and access resources.","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"B. Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Identity","correctReason":"AgentCore Identity helps agents securely authenticate and access resources. The key signal is an **existing agent needs managed runtime, memory, tools, and observability at scale**.","incorrectReasons":{"0":"Provides deterministic authorization policies for agent tool calls through AgentCore Gateway. Here, the deciding detail is an existing agent needs managed runtime, memory, tools, and observability at scale, so this option describes a different need.","2":"Stores, retrieves, and rotates secrets such as credentials; it is not a general encryption-key-management replacement for KMS. Here, the deciding detail is an existing agent needs managed runtime, memory, tools, and observability at scale, so this option describes a different need.","3":"Provides configurable GenAI safeguards such as content filters, denied topics, and sensitive-information controls. Here, the deciding detail is an existing agent needs managed runtime, memory, tools, and observability at scale, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"an **existing agent needs managed runtime, memory, tools, and observability at scale**","examTip":"Signal: an **existing agent needs managed runtime, memory, tools, and observability at scale**. Decision rule: AgentCore. Contrast: Strands Agents is an SDK for building agents; Bedrock provides managed FM access more broadly.","transferCheck":"If the team instead needed to train and tune custom ML models with broad lifecycle control, which AWS service would become the stronger fit?","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"an **existing agent needs managed runtime, memory, tools, and observability at scale**","think":"AgentCore","contrast":"Strands Agents is an SDK for building agents; Bedrock provides managed FM access more broadly."}},"status":"substantially revised","task":"5.1","taskName":"Explain methods to secure AI systems","difficulty":"moderate","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: AI Data Governance Strategies","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/AI-Data-Governance-Strategies/page"}},{"q":"A company wants deterministic authorization controls that restrict which gateway tools an AI agent is permitted to invoke. Which capability BEST addresses this requirement?","opts":["Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Identity","Amazon Bedrock Guardrails","AWS KMS","Policy in Amazon Bedrock AgentCore"],"ans":[3],"why":"AgentCore Policy provides controls over agent actions and tool use.","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"D. Policy in Amazon Bedrock AgentCore","correctReason":"AgentCore Policy provides controls over agent actions and tool use. The key signal is an **existing agent needs managed runtime, memory, tools, and observability at scale**.","incorrectReasons":{"0":"Helps agents securely obtain and use identities and credentials for AWS and third-party resources. Here, the deciding detail is an existing agent needs managed runtime, memory, tools, and observability at scale, so this option describes a different need.","1":"Provides configurable GenAI safeguards such as content filters, denied topics, and sensitive-information controls. Here, the deciding detail is an existing agent needs managed runtime, memory, tools, and observability at scale, so this option describes a different need.","2":"Creates and manages encryption keys used to protect data. Here, the deciding detail is an existing agent needs managed runtime, memory, tools, and observability at scale, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"an **existing agent needs managed runtime, memory, tools, and observability at scale**","examTip":"Signal: an **existing agent needs managed runtime, memory, tools, and observability at scale**. Decision rule: AgentCore. Contrast: Strands Agents is an SDK for building agents; Bedrock provides managed FM access more broadly.","transferCheck":"If the team instead needed to train and tune custom ML models with broad lifecycle control, which AWS service would become the stronger fit?","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"an **existing agent needs managed runtime, memory, tools, and observability at scale**","think":"AgentCore","contrast":"Strands Agents is an SDK for building agents; Bedrock provides managed FM access more broadly."}},"status":"substantially revised","task":"5.1","taskName":"Explain methods to secure AI systems","difficulty":"moderate","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: Best Practices for Secure Data Engineering","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/Best-Practices-for-Secure-Data-Engineering/page"}},{"q":"A customer assistant should answer only from approved documentation and the team wants to reduce unsupported factual claims. Which approach BEST addresses this requirement?","opts":["Fine-tune the model only on historical responses","Increase model temperature","RAG grounding with trusted sources plus output validation","Use a larger context window without retrieval"],"ans":[2],"why":"Grounding and validation reduce unsupported or hallucinated claims.","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"C. RAG grounding with trusted sources plus output validation","correctReason":"Grounding and validation reduce unsupported or hallucinated claims. The key signal is **claims must be supported by approved source documents**.","incorrectReasons":{"0":"Changes behavior using past examples but does not guarantee grounding in current approved facts. Here, the deciding detail is claims must be supported by approved source documents, so this option describes a different need.","1":"Controls generation randomness, not the primary requirement in this scenario. Here, the deciding detail is claims must be supported by approved source documents, so this option describes a different need.","3":"Increases context capacity but does not guarantee the model receives approved, relevant source evidence. Here, the deciding detail is claims must be supported by approved source documents, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"**claims must be supported by approved source documents**","examTip":"Signal: **claims must be supported by approved source documents**. Decision rule: ground with RAG and validate outputs. Contrast: simply enlarging or fine-tuning a model does not guarantee current source support.","transferCheck":"If the goal changed from using current external knowledge to changing the model's persistent behavior, which customization approach would become more relevant?","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"**claims must be supported by approved source documents**","think":"ground with RAG and validate outputs","contrast":"simply enlarging or fine-tuning a model does not guarantee current source support."}},"status":"substantially revised","task":"5.1","taskName":"Explain methods to secure AI systems","difficulty":"moderate","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: AI Data Governance Strategies","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/AI-Data-Governance-Strategies/page"}},{"q":"A security analyst needs an audit history showing which identities made AWS API calls and when. Which AWS service is MOST appropriate?","opts":["AWS CloudTrail","AWS Config","Amazon CloudWatch","AWS Audit Manager"],"ans":[0],"why":"CloudTrail records AWS API activity.","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"A. AWS CloudTrail","correctReason":"CloudTrail records AWS API activity. The key signal is **who called which AWS API and when**.","incorrectReasons":{"1":"Records resource configuration state and can evaluate it against compliance rules. Here, the deciding detail is who called which AWS API and when, so this option describes a different need.","2":"Provides monitoring, metrics, logs, and alarms but is not the primary API audit-history service. Here, the deciding detail is who called which AWS API and when, so this option describes a different need.","3":"Automates evidence collection and helps assess controls against audit frameworks. Here, the deciding detail is who called which AWS API and when, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"**who called which AWS API and when**","examTip":"Signal: **who called which AWS API and when**. Decision rule: CloudTrail. Contrast: Config records resource configuration/compliance state.","transferCheck":"If the question changed from 'who made the API call?' to 'is this resource configured according to policy?', which AWS service would become primary?","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"**who called which AWS API and when**","think":"CloudTrail","contrast":"Config records resource configuration/compliance state."}},"status":"substantially revised","task":"5.2","taskName":"Recognize governance and compliance regulations for AI systems","difficulty":"moderate","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: AWS Services for Governance and Compliance","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/AWS-Services-for-Governance-and-Compliance"}},{"q":"A governance team wants to record AWS resource configuration changes and continuously evaluate those resources against required configuration rules. Which AWS service is MOST appropriate?","opts":["AWS CloudTrail","AWS Audit Manager","AWS Trusted Advisor","AWS Config"],"ans":[3],"why":"AWS Config tracks configuration state and evaluates compliance.","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"D. AWS Config","correctReason":"AWS Config tracks configuration state and evaluates compliance. The key signal is **resource configuration history and rule-based compliance**.","incorrectReasons":{"0":"Records AWS API and account activity for audit and investigation. Here, the deciding detail is resource configuration history and rule-based compliance, so this option describes a different need.","1":"Automates evidence collection and helps assess controls against audit frameworks. Here, the deciding detail is resource configuration history and rule-based compliance, so this option describes a different need.","2":"Provides best-practice checks and recommendations across areas such as security, performance, resilience, and cost. Here, the deciding detail is resource configuration history and rule-based compliance, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"**resource configuration history and rule-based compliance**","examTip":"Signal: **resource configuration history and rule-based compliance**. Decision rule: Config. Contrast: CloudTrail records API activity; Audit Manager collects audit evidence.","transferCheck":"If the question changed from configuration compliance to 'which identity called this API?', which service would become primary?","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"**resource configuration history and rule-based compliance**","think":"Config","contrast":"CloudTrail records API activity; Audit Manager collects audit evidence."}},"status":"substantially revised","task":"5.2","taskName":"Recognize governance and compliance regulations for AI systems","difficulty":"moderate","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: AWS Services for Governance and Compliance","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/AWS-Services-for-Governance-and-Compliance"}},{"q":"An auditor asks for AWS compliance reports and agreements that the company can download for its compliance review. Which AWS service should the company use?","opts":["AWS Audit Manager","AWS Artifact","AWS Config","AWS CloudTrail"],"ans":[1],"why":"AWS Artifact provides compliance reports and agreements.","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"B. AWS Artifact","correctReason":"AWS Artifact provides compliance reports and agreements. The key signal is **download AWS compliance reports or agreements**.","incorrectReasons":{"0":"Automates evidence collection and helps assess controls against audit frameworks. Here, the deciding detail is download AWS compliance reports or agreements, so this option describes a different need.","2":"Records resource configuration state and can evaluate it against compliance rules. Here, the deciding detail is download AWS compliance reports or agreements, so this option describes a different need.","3":"Records AWS API and account activity for audit and investigation. Here, the deciding detail is download AWS compliance reports or agreements, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"**download AWS compliance reports or agreements**","examTip":"Signal: **download AWS compliance reports or agreements**. Decision rule: Artifact. Contrast: Audit Manager automates evidence collection for your audits.","transferCheck":"If the auditor wanted automated evidence collection against controls instead of downloadable AWS reports, which service would become the better fit?","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"**download AWS compliance reports or agreements**","think":"Artifact","contrast":"Audit Manager automates evidence collection for your audits."}},"status":"substantially revised","task":"5.2","taskName":"Recognize governance and compliance regulations for AI systems","difficulty":"moderate","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: AWS Services for Governance and Compliance","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/AWS-Services-for-Governance-and-Compliance"}},{"q":"A compliance team wants to automate evidence collection against a control framework instead of manually gathering evidence for every audit. Which AWS service is MOST appropriate?","opts":["AWS Artifact","AWS Config","AWS Audit Manager","AWS CloudTrail"],"ans":[2],"why":"AWS Audit Manager automates evidence collection for audits.","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"C. AWS Audit Manager","correctReason":"AWS Audit Manager automates evidence collection for audits. The key signal is **automated evidence collection against audit controls/frameworks**.","incorrectReasons":{"0":"Provides downloadable AWS compliance reports and agreements. Here, the deciding detail is automated evidence collection against audit controls/frameworks, so this option describes a different need.","1":"Records resource configuration state and can evaluate it against compliance rules. Here, the deciding detail is automated evidence collection against audit controls/frameworks, so this option describes a different need.","3":"Records AWS API and account activity for audit and investigation. Here, the deciding detail is automated evidence collection against audit controls/frameworks, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"**automated evidence collection against audit controls/frameworks**","examTip":"Signal: **automated evidence collection against audit controls/frameworks**. Decision rule: Audit Manager. Contrast: Artifact supplies AWS compliance documents.","transferCheck":"If the auditor only needed AWS compliance reports and agreements, which service would be the better fit?","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"**automated evidence collection against audit controls/frameworks**","think":"Audit Manager","contrast":"Artifact supplies AWS compliance documents."}},"status":"substantially revised","task":"5.2","taskName":"Recognize governance and compliance regulations for AI systems","difficulty":"moderate","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: AWS Services for Governance and Compliance","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/AWS-Services-for-Governance-and-Compliance"}},{"q":"A security team wants continuous vulnerability management for supported AWS workloads that host parts of an AI solution. Which service is MOST appropriate?","opts":["Amazon Inspector","Amazon Macie","AWS Config","AWS Trusted Advisor"],"ans":[0],"why":"Amazon Inspector helps identify vulnerabilities and exposure.","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"A. Amazon Inspector","correctReason":"Amazon Inspector helps identify vulnerabilities and exposure. The key signal is **vulnerability management for supported workloads**.","incorrectReasons":{"1":"Discovers and helps protect sensitive data, especially in Amazon S3. Here, the deciding detail is vulnerability management for supported workloads, so this option describes a different need.","2":"Records resource configuration state and can evaluate it against compliance rules. Here, the deciding detail is vulnerability management for supported workloads, so this option describes a different need.","3":"Provides best-practice checks and recommendations across areas such as security, performance, resilience, and cost. Here, the deciding detail is vulnerability management for supported workloads, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"**vulnerability management for supported workloads**","examTip":"Signal: **vulnerability management for supported workloads**. Decision rule: Inspector. Contrast: Macie discovers sensitive data; Config evaluates resource settings.","transferCheck":"","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"**vulnerability management for supported workloads**","think":"Inspector","contrast":"Macie discovers sensitive data; Config evaluates resource settings."}},"status":"substantially revised","task":"5.2","taskName":"Recognize governance and compliance regulations for AI systems","difficulty":"moderate","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: AWS Services for Governance and Compliance","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/AWS-Services-for-Governance-and-Compliance"}},{"q":"An architecture team wants AWS best-practice recommendations covering areas such as security, performance, resilience, and cost optimization. Which service is MOST appropriate?","opts":["AWS Well-Architected Tool","AWS Trusted Advisor","AWS Config","AWS Cost Explorer"],"ans":[1],"why":"Trusted Advisor provides AWS best-practice checks.","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"B. AWS Trusted Advisor","correctReason":"Trusted Advisor provides AWS best-practice checks. The key signal is **AWS best-practice recommendations across several pillars**.","incorrectReasons":{"0":"Helps review workloads against AWS Well-Architected Framework best practices through structured reviews. Here, the deciding detail is AWS best-practice recommendations across several pillars, so this option describes a different need.","2":"Records resource configuration state and can evaluate it against compliance rules. Here, the deciding detail is AWS best-practice recommendations across several pillars, so this option describes a different need.","3":"Analyzes AWS cost and usage data. Here, the deciding detail is AWS best-practice recommendations across several pillars, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"**AWS best-practice recommendations across several pillars**","examTip":"Signal: **AWS best-practice recommendations across several pillars**. Decision rule: Trusted Advisor. Contrast: Config evaluates explicit configuration rules; Cost Explorer focuses on spend analysis.","transferCheck":"","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"**AWS best-practice recommendations across several pillars**","think":"Trusted Advisor","contrast":"Config evaluates explicit configuration rules; Cost Explorer focuses on spend analysis."}},"status":"substantially revised","task":"5.2","taskName":"Recognize governance and compliance regulations for AI systems","difficulty":"moderate","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: AWS Services for Governance and Compliance","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/AWS-Services-for-Governance-and-Compliance"}},{"q":"A company stores confidential training data and sends it to model endpoints over the network. Which security control BEST protects the data in both locations?","opts":["Encrypt only data stored at rest","Use TLS in transit but leave stored data unencrypted","Rely on IAM permissions without encryption","Encrypt data at rest and in transit"],"ans":[3],"why":"Sensitive data should be encrypted both at rest and in transit.","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"D. Encrypt data at rest and in transit","correctReason":"Sensitive data should be encrypted both at rest and in transit. The key signal is data is sensitive **both while stored and while crossing the network**.","incorrectReasons":{"0":"Protects stored data but leaves network traffic unaddressed. Here, the deciding detail is data is sensitive both while stored and while crossing the network, so this option describes a different need.","1":"Protects network traffic but leaves stored sensitive data unaddressed. Here, the deciding detail is data is sensitive both while stored and while crossing the network, so this option describes a different need.","2":"Controls permissions rather than filtering generated content or discovering PII. Here, the deciding detail is data is sensitive both while stored and while crossing the network, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"data is sensitive **both while stored and while crossing the network**","examTip":"Signal: data is sensitive **both while stored and while crossing the network**. Decision rule: protect it at rest and in transit. Contrast: IAM limits access but does not replace encryption.","transferCheck":"","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"data is sensitive **both while stored and while crossing the network**","think":"protect it at rest and in transit","contrast":"IAM limits access but does not replace encryption."}},"status":"substantially revised","task":"5.1","taskName":"Explain methods to secure AI systems","difficulty":"moderate","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: Best Practices for Secure Data Engineering","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/Best-Practices-for-Secure-Data-Engineering/page"}},{"q":"A malicious user embeds instructions in a document that tell the model to ignore the application's trusted system instructions. Which GenAI security threat does this illustrate?","opts":["Prompt injection","Training-data poisoning","Data leakage","Hallucination"],"ans":[0],"why":"Prompt injection attempts to manipulate model behavior through adversarial instructions.","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"A. Prompt injection","correctReason":"Prompt injection attempts to manipulate model behavior through adversarial instructions. The key signal is **untrusted input tries to override trusted instructions**.","incorrectReasons":{"1":"Corrupts training data to influence learned model behavior, which is different from an inference-time prompt attack. Here, the deciding detail is untrusted input tries to override trusted instructions, so this option describes a different need.","2":"Is unauthorized or unintended disclosure of sensitive information. Here, the deciding detail is untrusted input tries to override trusted instructions, so this option describes a different need.","3":"Is a plausible-sounding but unsupported or fabricated output. Here, the deciding detail is untrusted input tries to override trusted instructions, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"**untrusted input tries to override trusted instructions**","examTip":"Signal: **untrusted input tries to override trusted instructions**. Decision rule: prompt injection/hijacking risk. Contrast: data poisoning attacks training data; jailbreaking is a related attempt to bypass model safeguards.","transferCheck":"","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"**untrusted input tries to override trusted instructions**","think":"prompt injection/hijacking risk","contrast":"data poisoning attacks training data; jailbreaking is a related attempt to bypass model safeguards."}},"status":"substantially revised","task":"5.1","taskName":"Explain methods to secure AI systems","difficulty":"moderate","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: AI Data Governance Strategies","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/AI-Data-Governance-Strategies/page"}},{"q":"A GenAI assistant sometimes includes confidential customer details in responses to users who are not authorized to see them. Which control objective should the team address FIRST?","opts":["Model distillation","Token cost optimization","Data leakage prevention and output validation","Vector similarity tuning"],"ans":[2],"why":"The primary security issue is preventing sensitive information from being disclosed in model output.","task":"5.1","difficulty":"moderate","status":"replaced","taskName":"Explain methods to secure AI systems","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"C. Data leakage prevention and output validation","correctReason":"The primary security issue is preventing sensitive information from being disclosed in model output. The key signal is information from the **holdout/test set enters training**.","incorrectReasons":{"0":"Trains a smaller model to approximate a larger teacher model for efficiency. Here, the deciding detail is information from the holdout/test set enters training, so this option describes a different need.","1":"Reduces spend but does not address confidential information appearing in outputs. Here, the deciding detail is information from the holdout/test set enters training, so this option describes a different need.","3":"Affects retrieval behavior but is not a direct data-leakage control. Here, the deciding detail is information from the holdout/test set enters training, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"information from the **holdout/test set enters training**","examTip":"Signal: information from the **holdout/test set enters training**. Decision rule: identify data leakage. Contrast: normal preprocessing learned only from training data is not leakage.","transferCheck":"","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"information from the **holdout/test set enters training**","think":"identify data leakage","contrast":"normal preprocessing learned only from training data is not leakage."}},"kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: AI Data Governance Strategies","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/AI-Data-Governance-Strategies/page"}},{"q":"A governance team must document the origin of training data and the transformations it underwent before being used by a model. Which governance concept is MOST relevant?","opts":["Data retention","Data residency","Model monitoring","Data lineage"],"ans":[3],"why":"Data lineage documents origin and transformations.","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"D. Data lineage","correctReason":"Data lineage documents origin and transformations. The key signal is **where data came from and how it changed**.","incorrectReasons":{"0":"Defines how long data or logs must be kept and when they should be disposed of. Here, the deciding detail is where data came from and how it changed, so this option describes a different need.","1":"Governs the geographic location where data is stored or processed. Here, the deciding detail is where data came from and how it changed, so this option describes a different need.","2":"Addresses production data/model quality monitoring rather than model documentation or one-time explainability. Here, the deciding detail is where data came from and how it changed, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"**where data came from and how it changed**","examTip":"Signal: **where data came from and how it changed**. Decision rule: data lineage. Contrast: retention is how long data is kept; residency is where it is stored/processed.","transferCheck":"If the question changed from data provenance to how long records must be kept, which governance concept would apply?","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"**where data came from and how it changed**","think":"data lineage","contrast":"retention is how long data is kept; residency is where it is stored/processed."}},"status":"substantially revised","task":"5.2","taskName":"Recognize governance and compliance regulations for AI systems","difficulty":"moderate","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: Source Citation and Data Lineage","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/Source-Citation-and-Data-Lineage"}},{"q":"A regulator requires AI interaction logs to be retained for seven years and then disposed of according to policy. Which governance concept is MOST relevant?","opts":["Data residency policy","Data retention policy","Data lineage","Model evaluation"],"ans":[1],"why":"Retention policies define how long records must be kept.","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"B. Data retention policy","correctReason":"Retention policies define how long records must be kept. The key signal is **keep logs/data for a defined period, then dispose according to policy**.","incorrectReasons":{"0":"Governs the geographic location where data is stored or processed. Here, the deciding detail is keep logs/data for a defined period, then dispose according to policy, so this option describes a different need.","2":"Tracks data origins, movement, and transformations. Here, the deciding detail is keep logs/data for a defined period, then dispose according to policy, so this option describes a different need.","3":"Supports evaluation of foundation models and related resources using automated or human evaluation workflows. Here, the deciding detail is keep logs/data for a defined period, then dispose according to policy, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"**keep logs/data for a defined period, then dispose according to policy**","examTip":"Signal: **keep logs/data for a defined period, then dispose according to policy**. Decision rule: retention. Contrast: residency concerns geography; lineage concerns provenance.","transferCheck":"","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"**keep logs/data for a defined period, then dispose according to policy**","think":"retention","contrast":"residency concerns geography; lineage concerns provenance."}},"status":"substantially revised","task":"5.2","taskName":"Recognize governance and compliance regulations for AI systems","difficulty":"moderate","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: Source Citation and Data Lineage","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/Source-Citation-and-Data-Lineage"}},{"q":"A law requires customer data used by an AI workload to remain within an approved geographic jurisdiction. Which governance concept is MOST relevant?","opts":["Data retention","Data lineage","Data residency","Encryption key rotation"],"ans":[2],"why":"Data residency governs where data is stored and processed.","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"C. Data residency","correctReason":"Data residency governs where data is stored and processed. The key signal is **data must remain in an approved geography**.","incorrectReasons":{"0":"Defines how long data or logs must be kept and when they should be disposed of. Here, the deciding detail is data must remain in an approved geography, so this option describes a different need.","1":"Tracks data origins, movement, and transformations. Here, the deciding detail is data must remain in an approved geography, so this option describes a different need.","3":"Is a key-management practice, not a rule about the geographic location of data. Here, the deciding detail is data must remain in an approved geography, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"**data must remain in an approved geography**","examTip":"Signal: **data must remain in an approved geography**. Decision rule: residency. Contrast: retention governs duration; encryption governs protection.","transferCheck":"If the question changed from geography to retention period, which governance concept would apply?","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"**data must remain in an approved geography**","think":"residency","contrast":"retention governs duration; encryption governs protection."}},"status":"substantially revised","task":"5.2","taskName":"Recognize governance and compliance regulations for AI systems","difficulty":"moderate","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: Source Citation and Data Lineage","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/Source-Citation-and-Data-Lineage"}},{"q":"A company uses managed AI services on AWS. According to the AWS shared responsibility model, which statement BEST describes the division of security responsibilities?","opts":["AWS manages all customer IAM policies and data classification","AWS secures the underlying cloud infrastructure; the customer remains responsible for applicable security in the cloud","The customer is responsible for physical AWS data-center security","AWS is responsible for all application-level access controls"],"ans":[1],"why":"AWS secures the cloud; customers secure their data, identities, configurations, and workloads according to the service model.","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"B. AWS secures the underlying cloud infrastructure; the customer remains responsible for applicable security in the cloud","correctReason":"AWS secures the cloud; customers secure their data, identities, configurations, and workloads according to the service model. The key signal is the question asks who secures **the cloud** versus security **in the cloud**.","incorrectReasons":{"0":"Predicts a discrete category or class. Here, the deciding detail is the question asks who secures the cloud versus security in the cloud, so this option describes a different need.","2":"Reverses the shared-responsibility model; AWS is responsible for physical infrastructure security. Here, the deciding detail is the question asks who secures the cloud versus security in the cloud, so this option describes a different need.","3":"Overstates AWS responsibility because customers configure identities, permissions, data access, and application controls. Here, the deciding detail is the question asks who secures the cloud versus security in the cloud, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"the question asks who secures **the cloud** versus security **in the cloud**","examTip":"Signal: the question asks who secures **the cloud** versus security **in the cloud**. Decision rule: AWS secures the underlying infrastructure; customers remain responsible for their data, identities, configurations, and workloads according to the services used. Contrast: AWS does not manage all customer IAM or data-classification decisions.","transferCheck":"","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"the question asks who secures **the cloud** versus security **in the cloud**","think":"AWS secures the underlying infrastructure; customers remain responsible for their data, identities, configurations, and workloads according to the services used","contrast":"AWS does not manage all customer IAM or data-classification decisions."}},"status":"substantially revised","task":"5.2","taskName":"Recognize governance and compliance regulations for AI systems","difficulty":"moderate","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: Best Practices for Secure Data Engineering","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/Best-Practices-for-Secure-Data-Engineering/page"}},{"q":"An AI governance program must stay effective as models, regulations, and business uses change. Which practice BEST supports this goal?","opts":["Perform governance review only before the first deployment","Stop monitoring once the model passes its initial evaluation","Establish a recurring governance review cadence and update controls as conditions change","Avoid changing policies after launch to maintain consistency"],"ans":[2],"why":"Governance requires recurring review, not a one-time assessment.","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"C. Establish a recurring governance review cadence and update controls as conditions change","correctReason":"Governance requires recurring review, not a one-time assessment. The key signal is **models, regulations, and business uses change over time**.","incorrectReasons":{"0":"Creates a one-time snapshot that becomes stale as risks, regulations, and systems change. Here, the deciding detail is models, regulations, and business uses change over time, so this option describes a different need.","1":"Removes ongoing oversight and is inconsistent with continuous governance. Here, the deciding detail is models, regulations, and business uses change over time, so this option describes a different need.","3":"Prevents governance from adapting to new risks, laws, or business uses. Here, the deciding detail is models, regulations, and business uses change over time, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"**models, regulations, and business uses change over time**","examTip":"Signal: **models, regulations, and business uses change over time**. Decision rule: governance needs a recurring review cadence. Contrast: a one-time prelaunch review becomes stale.","transferCheck":"","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"**models, regulations, and business uses change over time**","think":"governance needs a recurring review cadence","contrast":"a one-time prelaunch review becomes stale."}},"status":"substantially revised","task":"5.2","taskName":"Recognize governance and compliance regulations for AI systems","difficulty":"straightforward","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: AWS Services for Governance and Compliance","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/AWS-Services-for-Governance-and-Compliance"}},{"q":"A team wants to reduce the chance that a GenAI assistant presents unsupported claims as facts. Which TWO controls are MOST appropriate? (Select TWO.)","opts":["Ground responses with trusted sources","Validate outputs or use confidence checks","Increase generation temperature","Remove source citations","Increase maximum output tokens"],"ans":[0,1],"why":"Grounding and output validation directly help detect and mitigate unsupported outputs.","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"A. Ground responses with trusted sources / B. Validate outputs or use confidence checks","correctReason":"Grounding and output validation directly help detect and mitigate unsupported outputs. The key signal is the goal is **reduce unsupported factual claims**.","incorrectReasons":{"2":"Increases randomness and diversity; it does not add grounding, privacy controls, or governance. Here, the deciding detail is the goal is reduce unsupported factual claims, so this option describes a different need.","3":"Help users verify evidence; removing them weakens rather than strengthens grounding and traceability. Here, the deciding detail is the goal is reduce unsupported factual claims, so this option describes a different need.","4":"Allows longer responses but does not provide evidence or validate factual claims. Here, the deciding detail is the goal is reduce unsupported factual claims, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"the goal is **reduce unsupported factual claims**","examTip":"Signal: the goal is **reduce unsupported factual claims**. Decision rule: grounding plus output validation is stronger than sampling tweaks. Contrast: lower temperature can reduce variation but does not supply evidence.","transferCheck":"","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"the goal is **reduce unsupported factual claims**","think":"grounding plus output validation is stronger than sampling tweaks","contrast":"lower temperature can reduce variation but does not supply evidence."}},"status":"substantially revised","task":"5.1","taskName":"Explain methods to secure AI systems","difficulty":"challenging","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: Source Citation and Data Lineage","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/Source-Citation-and-Data-Lineage"}},{"q":"A team is preparing confidential enterprise data for an AI solution. Which TWO practices BEST support secure data engineering? (Select TWO.)","opts":["Apply data access controls","Assess data quality and integrity","Give every project member administrator access","Place credentials directly in prompts","Disable encryption to simplify data processing"],"ans":[0,1],"why":"Secure data engineering includes access control, data quality, and integrity.","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"A. Apply data access controls / B. Assess data quality and integrity","correctReason":"Secure data engineering includes access control, data quality, and integrity. The key signal is secure data engineering needs **controlled access and trustworthy data**.","incorrectReasons":{"2":"Violates least privilege and unnecessarily broadens access. Here, the deciding detail is secure data engineering needs controlled access and trustworthy data, so this option describes a different need.","3":"Risks secret exposure and is not a secure credential-management practice. Here, the deciding detail is secure data engineering needs controlled access and trustworthy data, so this option describes a different need.","4":"Weakens confidentiality and is inconsistent with secure data engineering. Here, the deciding detail is secure data engineering needs controlled access and trustworthy data, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"secure data engineering needs **controlled access and trustworthy data**","examTip":"Signal: secure data engineering needs **controlled access and trustworthy data**. Decision rule: combine access controls with data quality/integrity practices. Contrast: administrator access and secrets in prompts weaken security.","transferCheck":"","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"secure data engineering needs **controlled access and trustworthy data**","think":"combine access controls with data quality/integrity practices","contrast":"administrator access and secrets in prompts weaken security."}},"status":"substantially revised","task":"5.1","taskName":"Explain methods to secure AI systems","difficulty":"challenging","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: Best Practices for Secure Data Engineering","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/Best-Practices-for-Secure-Data-Engineering/page"}},{"q":"An auditor asks both who changed an AWS resource and whether the resulting resource configuration complies with policy. Which combination of AWS services BEST meets both needs?","opts":["AWS Audit Manager for activity history and AWS Artifact for configuration state","AWS CloudTrail for activity history and AWS Config for configuration/compliance state","Amazon Inspector for activity history and Amazon Macie for configuration state","AWS Trusted Advisor for activity history and AWS KMS for configuration state"],"ans":[1],"why":"CloudTrail records API activity, while Config tracks resource configuration and compliance state.","explanation":{"correctAnswer":"B. AWS CloudTrail for activity history and AWS Config for configuration/compliance state","correctReason":"CloudTrail records API activity, while Config tracks resource configuration and compliance state. The key signal is **who called which AWS API and when**.","incorrectReasons":{"0":"Automates evidence collection and helps assess controls against audit frameworks. Here, the deciding detail is who called which AWS API and when, so this option describes a different need.","2":"Discovers and helps protect sensitive data, especially in Amazon S3. Here, the deciding detail is who called which AWS API and when, so this option describes a different need.","3":"Creates and manages encryption keys used to protect data. Here, the deciding detail is who called which AWS API and when, so this option describes a different need."},"examClue":"**who called which AWS API and when**","examTip":"Signal: **who called which AWS API and when**. Decision rule: CloudTrail. Contrast: Config records resource configuration/compliance state.","transferCheck":"If the question changed from 'who made the API call?' to 'is this resource configured according to policy?', which AWS service would become primary?","rememberThis":"","takeaway":{"signal":"**who called which AWS API and when**","think":"CloudTrail","contrast":"Config records resource configuration/compliance state."}},"status":"substantially revised","task":"5.2","taskName":"Recognize governance and compliance regulations for AI systems","difficulty":"challenging","reference":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/ai-practitioner-01/ai-practitioner-01-domain5.html","kodekloud":{"title":"KodeKloud: AWS Services for Governance and Compliance","url":"https://notes.kodekloud.com/docs/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner/Security-Compliance-and-Governance-for-AI-Solutions/AWS-Services-for-Governance-and-Compliance"}}]};
